      "text": "Linear Algebra\n\nVector:\n    - In computer science perspective, an ordered list of numbers\n    - In physics, an arrow which is solely defined by it's length and direction. It doesn't matter where it starts\n    - In LA, an arrow that is anchored at origin. The coordiantes tell you how far to step along each direction to reach the arrow's tip.\n\nScalar: Any real number, C. when multiplied with vector, it can stretch, shrink or reverse direction of the vector.\n\nVector Addition: Placing vectors tip-to-tail to get the combined effect of the addition (v + w).\n\nLinear Dependence: A set of vectors is called Linearly dependent, iff atleast one vector can be represented as sum of scaled versions of other vectors in the set.\n    i.e {v1, v2, v3} is linearly dependent, iff c1v1 = c2v2 + c3v3, where c1 != 0.\n\n-> If a set of vectors is not linearly dependent, then it is linearly independent.\n\n-> c1v1 + c2v2 + c3v3 + ... + cnvn = 0.\n    {v1, v2, v3, ... , vn} is Linearly dependent iff atleast one of ci != 0.\n    {v1, v2, v3, ... , vn} is Linearly Independent iff the trivial solution c1 = c2 = ... = cn = 0 is the only trivial solution that satisifies the equation.\n\nSpan: Span of a set of vectors is the set of all possible vectors that can be represented as linear combinations.\n\n-> Span of a single vector in 2D Space: A 1D line passing through origin.\n-> Span of two non-collinear vectors in 2D Space: The entire 2D space (R2)\n-> Span of two collinear vectors in 2D Space: A 1D line passing through origin.\n\nBasis: Basis of a vector space is the set of vectors that are linearly independent and can span entire space.\n\nDimension: is the number of vectors in the basis of a space. R2 has 2 dimension, coz, the basis has 2 vectors.\n\nUnit Vectors: The set of n vectors in Rn space, where in each vector only one co-ordinate has a value 1 and rest has a value 0. They also form a basis.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Linear Algebra\n\nVector:\n    - In computer science perspective, an ordered list of numbers\n    - In physics, an arrow which is solely defined by it's length and direction. It doesn't matter where it starts\n    - In LA, an arrow that is anchored at origin. The coordiantes tell you how far to step along each direction to reach the arrow's tip.\n\nScalar: Any real number, C. when multiplied with vector, it can stretch, shrink or reverse direction of the vector.\n\nVector Addition: Placing vectors tip-to-tail to get the combined effect of the addition (v + w).\n\nLinear Dependence: A set of vectors is called Linearly dependent, iff atleast one vector can be represented as sum of scaled versions of other vectors in the set.\n    i.e {v1, v2, v3} is linearly dependent, iff c1v1 = c2v2 + c3v3, where c1 != 0.\n\n-> If a set of vectors is not linearly dependent, then it is linearly independent.\n\n-> c1v1 + c2v2 + c3v3 + ... + cnvn = 0.\n    {v1, v2, v3, ... , vn} is Linearly dependent iff atleast one of ci != 0.\n    {v1, v2, v3, ... , vn} is Linearly Independent iff the trivial solution c1 = c2 = ... = cn = 0 is the only trivial solution that satisifies the equation.\n\nSpan: Span of a set of vectors is the set of all possible vectors that can be represented as linear combinations.\n\n-> Span of a single vector in 2D Space: A 1D line passing through origin.\n-> Span of two non-collinear vectors in 2D Space: The entire 2D space (R2)\n-> Span of two collinear vectors in 2D Space: A 1D line passing through origin.\n\nBasis: Basis of a vector space is the set of vectors that are linearly independent and can span entire space.\n\nDimension: is the number of vectors in the basis of a space. R2 has 2 dimension, coz, the basis has 2 vectors.\n\nUnit Vectors: The set of n vectors in Rn space, where in each vector only one co-ordinate has a value 1 and rest has a value 0. They also form a basis.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
