      "text": "System of Linear Equations:\n\n-> Given the following form of system of linear equations, Ax = b:\n    \n    a1   a3     x1      b1\n              x       =\n    a2  a4     x2      b2\n    \n    Here A represents a matrix, x is the unknown and b is result.\n\n\nWe can interpret this in two ways:    \n1) Row Picture: where each row represents an equation.\n    - a1x1 + a3x2 = b1  and  a2x1 + a4x2 = b2.\n    - This is the form that correlates to the Concept of \"System of Linear equations\".\n    - We are given a set of linear equations, find x1 and x2 that satisfy all the equations. i.e. solve the system.\n    - There are two unknowns, this can be interpreted as the point where two lines intersect in a 2d space.\n    - If there are three unknowns and three equations, this can be interpreted as the point where three planes intersect in a 3d space.\n\n2) Column Picture: where each column represents a vector.\n    - It is basically asking to find whether vector b is a linear combination of columns/vectors of A. i.e. is there a vector x that can satisfy Ax = b.\n    - For Ax = b, If b is a Linear combination of A, Then there always exist a solution. Other wise no solution exist.\n    - If the columns are LI, then there exists only ONE solution, otherwise infinte solutions exist.\n\n\nSolving System of Linear Equations(Gaussian Elimination):\n\n-> Make Augumented matrix with A and b. Now you can do row operations to convert the augumented matrix into ROW ECHELON FORM.\n    The operations must involve the row that we are modifying on the RHS side of the operation.\n\n-> Row Echelon Form:\n    - The matrix A part of the augumented matrix must be in the following form:\n    - Zero rows must be at the bottom.\n    - The leading non zero entry, i.e. the left most non zero entry of each row, called the PIVOT, must be to the right of every leading non-zero entry of the above rows.\n    \n-> We can interpret the row echelon form of the  matrix like this:\n    - The columns where there is a pivot are called Pivot Columns or Pivot variables. These represent the columns that are linearly independent.\n    - The columns where there is no pivot are called Free variables. These represent the columns that are linearly dependent.\n    - Rank of a matrix = Number of Linearly Independent columns = Number of Pivot variables\n    - If, Number of free variables > 0, then the system can have infinte solutions.\n\n-> Then you can substitute from bottom to top, the unknowns/variables in each row to get the solution/s. If at all you encounter a situation, where the row is all zeros but \n    you have a constant in the augumented part, then that means the solution doesn't exist. That means, we the system is saying b is a valid transformation of some vector. \n    But it is not. I think the only case where this happens, is when b is given as a vector that doesn't reside in the reduced dimensional space of the transformation.\n\nColumn Space: is the set of all possible vectors that can be formed by the linear combination of Linearly indepedent columns in the matrix. Rank is the # dimensions of column space\nNull Space or Kernel: is the set of all input vectors that will be squished to origin if they are transformed by the matrix A. NULLITY is the # dimensions of the Null space.\n\nRank-Nullity theorem: Given a transformation T: V -> W, the number of dimensions of the domain of V = # dimensions of column space + # dimensions of null space\n    \n\nAnalysing Matrices:\n\n-> Square Matrices n x n:\n    - These represent n vectors in n-dimensional space.\n    - If Rank = n, i.e. full rank, then that means there is a unique solution for the system involving the matrix.\n    - If Rank < n, then that means if solution exists then they can be infinite or the solution may not even exist.\n    - The # dimensions of column space is equal to Rank, because, Rank basically gives the number of basis vectors in the space.\n       and number of basis vectors is equal to the number of dimensions that we can use to plot vectors in the space.\n    - If we consider a 3x3 matrix, and Rank is 3, then we can find the linear transformation of every vector in the space.\n    ******- But, if the Rank is 2, this means, a dimension is lost and we only have a PLANE that can store the vectors. And only those\n      original vectors which when transformed fall into this plane, surivive, rest get squisted to origin(null space).\n    ******- If Rank is 1, this means, two dimensions are lost, all that we have is a line of vectors, those vectors which land on this line\n      after transformation survive. The rest are coverged into null space.\n\n-> m x n, where m < n\n    - Square matrices are transformations that take vectors in an n-dimensional space as input and gives the output vectors which have the same-\n      number of dimensions. Although some vectors can get squished into null space, nevertheless it gives a valid 3 dimensional vector which we can plot\n      in a 3d space, i.e. origin.\n\n    - But, when m < n, then the matrix takes higher - dimensional vectors as input and outputs their transformed versions in a lower-dimensional space. eg: 3 x 5 takes \n      5 - dimensional vectors and outputs 3 - dimensional vectors. Here, the squishing of two dimensions is explicit, we know we are trying to plot 5 dimensional vectors\n      in a 3 - dimensional space.\n\n    - If we were to assume there are 3 linearly independent vectors in the matrix, then the rank/# dimensions in column space is 3. And since, we are losing 2 dimensions\n      the number of free variables/# number of linearly dependent columns/ # dimensions of null space is 2. The rank will always be less than or equal to m.\n\n-> m x n, where m > n\n    - This is the case, when we are taking a lower-dimensional vector and transforming it into a vector in a higher-dimensional space.\n    - What can be rank of such a matrix? The rank of such a matrix will always be less than or equal to n. This is because, it is impossible to create a new dimensional component for the original\n      vector out of thin air, just because it is being transformed into a higher dimensional one. A paper if placed in a room is still a paper, but it resides in the 3d space as a plane\n      containing only those vectors that are present on it.\n       \n\nConsider the equation Ax = 0. What does the solution of such equation tell?\n    - It gives the linearly independent vectors(if they exist) which form the null space of the transformation A.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "System of Linear Equations:\n\n-> Given the following form of system of linear equations, Ax = b:\n    \n    a1   a3     x1      b1\n              x       =\n    a2  a4     x2      b2\n    \n    Here A represents a matrix, x is the unknown and b is result.\n\n\nWe can interpret this in two ways:    \n1) Row Picture: where each row represents an equation.\n    - a1x1 + a3x2 = b1  and  a2x1 + a4x2 = b2.\n    - This is the form that correlates to the Concept of \"System of Linear equations\".\n    - We are given a set of linear equations, find x1 and x2 that satisfy all the equations. i.e. solve the system.\n    - There are two unknowns, this can be interpreted as the point where two lines intersect in a 2d space.\n    - If there are three unknowns and three equations, this can be interpreted as the point where three planes intersect in a 3d space.\n\n2) Column Picture: where each column represents a vector.\n    - It is basically asking to find whether vector b is a linear combination of columns/vectors of A. i.e. is there a vector x that can satisfy Ax = b.\n    - For Ax = b, If b is a Linear combination of A, Then there always exist a solution. Other wise no solution exist.\n    - If the columns are LI, then there exists only ONE solution, otherwise infinte solutions exist.\n\n\nSolving System of Linear Equations(Gaussian Elimination):\n\n-> Make Augumented matrix with A and b. Now you can do row operations to convert the augumented matrix into ROW ECHELON FORM.\n    The operations must involve the row that we are modifying on the RHS side of the operation.\n\n-> Row Echelon Form:\n    - The matrix A part of the augumented matrix must be in the following form:\n    - Zero rows must be at the bottom.\n    - The leading non zero entry, i.e. the left most non zero entry of each row, called the PIVOT, must be to the right of every leading non-zero entry of the above rows.\n    \n-> We can interpret the row echelon form of the  matrix like this:\n    - The columns where there is a pivot are called Pivot Columns or Pivot variables. These represent the columns that are linearly independent.\n    - The columns where there is no pivot are called Free variables. These represent the columns that are linearly dependent.\n    - Rank of a matrix = Number of Linearly Independent columns = Number of Pivot variables\n    - If, Number of free variables > 0, then the system can have infinte solutions.\n\n-> Then you can substitute from bottom to top, the unknowns/variables in each row to get the solution/s. If at all you encounter a situation, where the row is all zeros but \n    you have a constant in the augumented part, then that means the solution doesn't exist. That means, we the system is saying b is a valid transformation of some vector. \n    But it is not. I think the only case where this happens, is when b is given as a vector that doesn't reside in the reduced dimensional space of the transformation.\n\nColumn Space: is the set of all possible vectors that can be formed by the linear combination of Linearly indepedent columns in the matrix. Rank is the # dimensions of column space\nNull Space or Kernel: is the set of all input vectors that will be squished to origin if they are transformed by the matrix A. NULLITY is the # dimensions of the Null space.\n\nRank-Nullity theorem: Given a transformation T: V -> W, the number of dimensions of the domain of V = # dimensions of column space + # dimensions of null space\n    \n\nAnalysing Matrices:\n\n-> Square Matrices n x n:\n    - These represent n vectors in n-dimensional space.\n    - If Rank = n, i.e. full rank, then that means there is a unique solution for the system involving the matrix.\n    - If Rank < n, then that means if solution exists then they can be infinite or the solution may not even exist.\n    - The # dimensions of column space is equal to Rank, because, Rank basically gives the number of basis vectors in the space.\n       and number of basis vectors is equal to the number of dimensions that we can use to plot vectors in the space.\n    - If we consider a 3x3 matrix, and Rank is 3, then we can find the linear transformation of every vector in the space.\n    ******- But, if the Rank is 2, this means, a dimension is lost and we only have a PLANE that can store the vectors. And only those\n      original vectors which when transformed fall into this plane, surivive, rest get squisted to origin(null space).\n    ******- If Rank is 1, this means, two dimensions are lost, all that we have is a line of vectors, those vectors which land on this line\n      after transformation survive. The rest are coverged into null space.\n\n-> m x n, where m < n\n    - Square matrices are transformations that take vectors in an n-dimensional space as input and gives the output vectors which have the same-\n      number of dimensions. Although some vectors can get squished into null space, nevertheless it gives a valid 3 dimensional vector which we can plot\n      in a 3d space, i.e. origin.\n\n    - But, when m < n, then the matrix takes higher - dimensional vectors as input and outputs their transformed versions in a lower-dimensional space. eg: 3 x 5 takes \n      5 - dimensional vectors and outputs 3 - dimensional vectors. Here, the squishing of two dimensions is explicit, we know we are trying to plot 5 dimensional vectors\n      in a 3 - dimensional space.\n\n    - If we were to assume there are 3 linearly independent vectors in the matrix, then the rank/# dimensions in column space is 3. And since, we are losing 2 dimensions\n      the number of free variables/# number of linearly dependent columns/ # dimensions of null space is 2. The rank will always be less than or equal to m.\n\n-> m x n, where m > n\n    - This is the case, when we are taking a lower-dimensional vector and transforming it into a vector in a higher-dimensional space.\n    - What can be rank of such a matrix? The rank of such a matrix will always be less than or equal to n. This is because, it is impossible to create a new dimensional component for the original\n      vector out of thin air, just because it is being transformed into a higher dimensional one. A paper if placed in a room is still a paper, but it resides in the 3d space as a plane\n      containing only those vectors that are present on it.\n       \n\nConsider the equation Ax = 0. What does the solution of such equation tell?\n    - It gives the linearly independent vectors(if they exist) which form the null space of the transformation A.",
