      "text": "Determinants and Inverses:\n\n-> Determinant Geometrically represents the factor by which the measurement of space is scaled after a transformation.\n-> In 2D, it gives the factor by which area of a unit cell in the grid, scaled.\n-> In 3D, it gives the factor by which volume of a unit cube in the 3 dimensional space scaled.\n-> It is common sense to notice that, the transformation increases or decreases the space only if the transformation preserves all dimensions in the input space.\n-> Also, it doesn't make sense to think about the increase in space if we transform a higher dimesion to lower or vice-versa. So, determinants are only valid for SQUARE MATRICES.\n-> But, it is not to say a transformation with # dimensions of column space less than n is invalid. It is a valid transformation because the output dimension is still preserved.\n    But some dimensions are shrinked to origin.\n-> If a transformation transforms 2D space onto a line or a point, or 3D space onto a plane or line or point, then the determinant of the transformation is ZERO.\n-> The determinant can be negative. the minus represents the change in orientation of the space after transformation. The magnitude gives the factor by which the\n    space is scaled.\n-> So, the determinant of a matrix/transformation is zero, if the rank is < n.\n\n\n-> Inverse represents the UNDO of a transformation. A * A^-1 = I = A^-1 * A.\n-> We can't have inverse of transformations whose determinant is zero. This is because, we cannot undo a squish operation. The data to reconstruct the\n    squished dimensions is lost. \n-> So, inverse don't exist, if the rank of the matrix isn't full.\n\n\nNow let's see the mathematical properties and calculations and derivations.\n\nAxiom-kind of properties of Determinants:\n1) det(I) = 1, I is the indentity matrix\n2) If you swap two rows or two columns in the det, then the sign of the det changes.\n3) Linearity is applied one row at a time.\n\nOther properties:\n4) If two rows of A are equal or two columns are equal, det(A) = 0\n5) If mutliple of a row is added to another row or a multiple of a column is added to another column, the det is unchanged.\n6) Matrix with a row of zeros or a column of zeros has det equal to zero\n7) Determinant of a diagonal matrix is \"product of the diagonal elements\"\n8) Determinant of a upper/lower triangular matrix is \"product of the diagonal elements\".\n9) det(AB) = det(A)*det(B) = det(BA)\n10) det(A^T) = det(A)\n11) det(kA) = k^n * det(A)\n\n\nMethods to calculate Determinant:\n\n1) Pivot Method\n    - convert the DETERMINANT into row echelon form.\n    - this gives a upper triangular determinant.\n    - det is product of diagonal elements\n\n2) Big Formula Method\n    - There are n! combinations of products that compose the determinant.\n    - you can chose n elements from the first row, for each of those elements you can choose, n-1 elements from the second row, for each of the pair you can choose\n      n-2 elements from the third row, for each of the triple you can choose, n - 3 element in the fourth row and so on. So, a total of n! combinations.\n    - Each term has a sign of + or -. you can know it by determining how many swaps you would need to form a diagonal matrix, if you place the choosen elements in their\n      original positions and make the rest of the elements zero. If even number of swaps are required, then the sign is +ve, otherwise -Ve.\n    - The choosing strategy is to never pick an element of the same column that you have already picked from in the top rows.\n\n3) Cofactor Method.\n    - You can find the cofactors for all elements present in a row or a column. and multiply each with the respective element in the row or column. and sum the products.\n    - So, if first row was chosen, in Anxn, then, det(A) = a11*C11 + a12*C12 + a13*C13 + ... + a1n*C1n.\n    \n    Recursive Steps:    \n\n        How to find Co-factors?\n    \n        - Eliminate the row and column of the element that you are find co-factor for.\n        - Find the determinant of the remaining determinant.\n        \n    \n        How to find Determinant?\n        \n        - If, the dimension is 2x2, determinant is ad - bc. RETURN\n        - For each element in the row, Find the co-factors.\n        - multiply element with co-factor and sum the products.\n\n    - sign of each co-factor is (-1)^(i+j), where i and j are the row and column of position of element whose co-factor you are finding.\n\n-> PIVOT METHOD IS THE EASISET AND SAVES TIME. COFACTOR METHOD IS FAIRLY SIMPLE FOR 3X3 MATRICES. BIG FORMULA MIGHT GET CUMBERSOME.\n      \n\nHow to calculate Inverse of A?\n\n- Inverse of A = Adjoint(A) * 1/det(A)\n- Adjoint(A) is the Transpose of Co-factor matrix.\n- Co-factor matrix is the matrix formed when you replace the elements with their respective co-factors.\n\n-> A * Adj(A) = |A| * I\n\n\nCramer's Rule:\n\n- Given Ax = b, This can be written as x = Inv(A) * b\n- This gives x to be, [det(A1)/det(A), det(A2)/det(A), det(A3)/det(A)]\n- This is because x1 = b1*C11 + b2*C21 + b3*C31, which is nothing but the determinant of matrix A, where the first column is replaced by [b1, b2, b3]\n  Using same explanantion, A2 is A, where the second column is replaced by [b1, b2, b3] and A3 is A, where the third column is replaced by [b1, b2, b3].\n\n\nFor solving Ax = b, if you have A Inverse, just multiply it with b. If not, Cramer's is expensive and time taking. guassian is still best overall.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Determinants and Inverses:\n\n-> Determinant Geometrically represents the factor by which the measurement of space is scaled after a transformation.\n-> In 2D, it gives the factor by which area of a unit cell in the grid, scaled.\n-> In 3D, it gives the factor by which volume of a unit cube in the 3 dimensional space scaled.\n-> It is common sense to notice that, the transformation increases or decreases the space only if the transformation preserves all dimensions in the input space.\n-> Also, it doesn't make sense to think about the increase in space if we transform a higher dimesion to lower or vice-versa. So, determinants are only valid for SQUARE MATRICES.\n-> But, it is not to say a transformation with # dimensions of column space less than n is invalid. It is a valid transformation because the output dimension is still preserved.\n    But some dimensions are shrinked to origin.\n-> If a transformation transforms 2D space onto a line or a point, or 3D space onto a plane or line or point, then the determinant of the transformation is ZERO.\n-> The determinant can be negative. the minus represents the change in orientation of the space after transformation. The magnitude gives the factor by which the\n    space is scaled.\n-> So, the determinant of a matrix/transformation is zero, if the rank is < n.\n\n\n-> Inverse represents the UNDO of a transformation. A * A^-1 = I = A^-1 * A.\n-> We can't have inverse of transformations whose determinant is zero. This is because, we cannot undo a squish operation. The data to reconstruct the\n    squished dimensions is lost. \n-> So, inverse don't exist, if the rank of the matrix isn't full.\n\n\nNow let's see the mathematical properties and calculations and derivations.\n\nAxiom-kind of properties of Determinants:\n1) det(I) = 1, I is the indentity matrix\n2) If you swap two rows or two columns in the det, then the sign of the det changes.\n3) Linearity is applied one row at a time.\n\nOther properties:\n4) If two rows of A are equal or two columns are equal, det(A) = 0\n5) If mutliple of a row is added to another row or a multiple of a column is added to another column, the det is unchanged.\n6) Matrix with a row of zeros or a column of zeros has det equal to zero\n7) Determinant of a diagonal matrix is \"product of the diagonal elements\"\n8) Determinant of a upper/lower triangular matrix is \"product of the diagonal elements\".\n9) det(AB) = det(A)*det(B) = det(BA)\n10) det(A^T) = det(A)\n11) det(kA) = k^n * det(A)\n\n\nMethods to calculate Determinant:\n\n1) Pivot Method\n    - convert the DETERMINANT into row echelon form.\n    - this gives a upper triangular determinant.\n    - det is product of diagonal elements\n\n2) Big Formula Method\n    - There are n! combinations of products that compose the determinant.\n    - you can chose n elements from the first row, for each of those elements you can choose, n-1 elements from the second row, for each of the pair you can choose\n      n-2 elements from the third row, for each of the triple you can choose, n - 3 element in the fourth row and so on. So, a total of n! combinations.\n    - Each term has a sign of + or -. you can know it by determining how many swaps you would need to form a diagonal matrix, if you place the choosen elements in their\n      original positions and make the rest of the elements zero. If even number of swaps are required, then the sign is +ve, otherwise -Ve.\n    - The choosing strategy is to never pick an element of the same column that you have already picked from in the top rows.\n\n3) Cofactor Method.\n    - You can find the cofactors for all elements present in a row or a column. and multiply each with the respective element in the row or column. and sum the products.\n    - So, if first row was chosen, in Anxn, then, det(A) = a11*C11 + a12*C12 + a13*C13 + ... + a1n*C1n.\n    \n    Recursive Steps:    \n\n        How to find Co-factors?\n    \n        - Eliminate the row and column of the element that you are find co-factor for.\n        - Find the determinant of the remaining determinant.\n        \n    \n        How to find Determinant?\n        \n        - If, the dimension is 2x2, determinant is ad - bc. RETURN\n        - For each element in the row, Find the co-factors.\n        - multiply element with co-factor and sum the products.\n\n    - sign of each co-factor is (-1)^(i+j), where i and j are the row and column of position of element whose co-factor you are finding.\n\n-> PIVOT METHOD IS THE EASISET AND SAVES TIME. COFACTOR METHOD IS FAIRLY SIMPLE FOR 3X3 MATRICES. BIG FORMULA MIGHT GET CUMBERSOME.\n      \n\nHow to calculate Inverse of A?\n\n- Inverse of A = Adjoint(A) * 1/det(A)\n- Adjoint(A) is the Transpose of Co-factor matrix.\n- Co-factor matrix is the matrix formed when you replace the elements with their respective co-factors.\n\n-> A * Adj(A) = |A| * I\n\n\nCramer's Rule:\n\n- Given Ax = b, This can be written as x = Inv(A) * b\n- This gives x to be, [det(A1)/det(A), det(A2)/det(A), det(A3)/det(A)]\n- This is because x1 = b1*C11 + b2*C21 + b3*C31, which is nothing but the determinant of matrix A, where the first column is replaced by [b1, b2, b3]\n  Using same explanantion, A2 is A, where the second column is replaced by [b1, b2, b3] and A3 is A, where the third column is replaced by [b1, b2, b3].\n\n\nFor solving Ax = b, if you have A Inverse, just multiply it with b. If not, Cramer's is expensive and time taking. guassian is still best overall.",
