      "text": "Eigen Values and Eigen Vectors\n\n-> when a matrix acts on space, it usually stretches, squishes and rotates vectors off their original line\n    of direction. Eigen values and Eigen vectors identify the rare, invariant directions where no rotation\n    occurs.\n\n-> For Almost every vector x in a space, applying a transformation Ax knocks the vector onto a completely\n    new line of span. However, every transformation has special directions where vectors stay on their exact\n    original line of span.\n\n-> The vectors that stay on their line are eigenvectors.\n-> The factor by which these vectors stretch, shrink, flip or stay unchanged is the eigen value.\n\n-> Ax = lambda*x   =>   (A - lambda*I)x = 0  => det(A - lambda*I) = 0.\n-> Solve the polynomial for lambda. substitute each lambda in (A - lambda*I)x = 0. solve to get eigen vectors corresponding\n    to that lambda. i.e. The resulting basis vectors of this null space form the eigen space for the substituted eigen value.\n\n\n-> As long as the transformation doesn't rotate all the axis of the space in the same direction. A matrix\n    will always have infinte number of eigen vectors.\n\n-> Given the following characteristic equation:\n    (lambda - 1)^5 (lambda - 4)^2 (lambda - 5)^3 = 0\n    \n    - The eigen vectors of two different lambdas are always linearly independent.\n    - For a repeating lambda, the number of linearly indepedent eigen vectors is atmost the power of \n      it's component in the characteristic equation.\n    - So, In total for the matrix with this characteristic equation, there can be atmost 10 LI eigen vectors.\n      and atleast 3 LI eigen vectors. atmost n and atleast # distinct eigen values.\n    \n-> For every eigenvalue lambda, Geometric Multiplicity (GM) is the # LI eigen vectors corresponding to lambda.\n    Algebraic Multiplicity (AM) is the number of times lambda is repeating. GM <= AM.\n\n-> REAL Symmetric Matrices always have AM = GM, so they have n LI eigen vectors which are also orthogonal. The number of distinct eigen values don't matter.\n\n-> Determinant of a matrix A, is equal to the product of eigen values(including repeated values)\n-> Sum of elements in main diagonal of A, trace(A) is equal to sum of eigen values(including repeated values)\n\n-> if zero is an eigen value of a matrix A, Then product of eigen values = det(A). Which implies Det(A) = 0\n-> We can only tell whether the rank of matrix A is full or not from the info of eigen values, that too from the\n    specific info of whether zero is an eigen value of the matrix or not.\n-> But if we were given the GEMOETRIC MULTIPLICTY of lambda = 0, then the rank of matrix a is, (n - GM of zero eigen value)\n    This is because solving (A - lambda*I)x = 0, for lamdba = 0, gives the dimensions of null space of A, which is same \n    as number of LI eigen vectors for zero eigen value.\n\n\nCayley-Hamilton Theorem:\n-> It says, Every square matrix satisfies it's own characteristic equation.\n-> You can use, this to find powers of matrices, even inverse powers. \n\n-> AB and BA share same non zero eigen values, in case they are non-square, if they are square they have same eigen values.\n-> If v is an eigen vector of AB with eigenvalue != 0, then Bv is the eigen vector of BA.\n-> If u is an eigen vector of BA with eigenvalue != 0, then Au is the eigen vector of AB.\n\n-> If A has an eigen value lambda, then A^k has an eigen value lamda^k, where k is an integer.\n\n\nChange of Basis:\n\n-> We have seen that a linear transformation represents, change in the input space of vectors. this change is because of the change in basis vectors. And so far, the matrix A always contains the basis \n    which is represented in terms of standard basis.\n-> So, we can think of the transformation matrix, A as taking inputs of vector space, whose basis vectors are standard basis, and outputting vectors after the transformation\n    which are also represented in terms of standard basis.\n-> Since, we are so accustomed to these kind of transformations, we got blind about the fact that a matrix can take vectors which are represented by non standard basis\n    and can also output vectors in terms of bases which are different from the bases of input vectors.\n\n-> So, A matrix M, which take inputs from bases B and outputs the vectors in terms of bases B' would be of the form:\n    B'[M]B. The columns of M are the transformed basis of B. so columns are represented in terms of bases of B'.\n    The opposite of this, a transformation that takes inputs from B' and converts them to B, would be B[inverse of M]B'.\n\n-> How about representing transformation matrices themselves in terms of other bases?\n    - Let M be a transformation matrix that takes inputs from vector space B and outputs vectors in terms of same basis. We can represent this matrix interms of a different bases P.\n    - Then P M P^-1 represents the transformation matrix interms of bases of P. P^-1 has columns that are basis of P represented interms of basis of B, and P has columns which are basis of B \n      represented interms of P.\n    - for a given vector v in basis of P, when multiplied by above product of matrices, these operations are performed:\n      1) v is converted to the form of bases of M.\n      2) Then the transformation M is performed on the vector.\n      3) This transformed vector is converted back to the basis of P.\n\n    - So, the product of matrices itself gives the transformation matrix interms of bases of P.\n\n\nDiagonalization:\n\n-> Consider a transformation Anxn, if it has n eigen vectors. We can do something interesting.\n-> Note that the transformation doesn't change the direction of lines along the eigen vectors. So, if we represent the transformation in terms of \n    n eigen vectors, i.e. change the baisis to eigenspace, the transformation is just stretching/shrinking the eigen basis. There is no cross talk from one\n    axis/basis to other.\n-> So, we can say, A = Q^-1 D Q, This is what's happening:\n    - Q has columns that are standard basis represented interms of eigen basis. so input vector is first converted into eigenbasis.\n    - Then the transformation is applied by multiplying the input vector with D.\n    - Q^-1 has columns that are eigen basis represented in terms of standard basis. so input vector is converted back into standard basis.\n-> This can also be used to find powers of A, A^k = Q^-1 D^k Q\n\n\nLU Decomposition (Factorization):\n\n-> Matrix A, is represented as the product of a lower triangular matrix and upper triangular matrix.\n    A = LU, the diagonal elements of L are all 1.\n-> Finding Ax = b. It takes O(n^3) to convert A|b into row echelon form. So, everytime you want to find a solution for bi,\n    this needs to be performed.\n\n-> We can optimize this by finding L and U once, which takes O(n^3). And then solve two \n    triangular systems using forward substitution. Ux = y and Ly = b.\n-> Solving triangular systems takes only O(n^2) steps.\n\n-> Determinants can also be calculated fast. det(A) = det(L)*det(U)\n-> Inv(A) = Inv(U) * Inv(L)\n\nSteps to find LU decomposition:\n\n-> U is the resultant matrix after converting A into row echelon form.\n-> L's lower half is determined as follows:\n    - every time you make an element in the lower half of A, zero, you do the following\n      operations: Ri -> Ri + kRj. \n    - Insert k as the element in L at the same place as the element that became zero.\n\n\nSpecial Matrices:\n\n1) Transpose:\n    - (A^-1)^T = (A^T)^-1\n    - (A + B)^T = A^T + B^T\n    - (AB)^T = B^T * A^T\n    - (kA)^T = k * A^T\n\n2) Inverse:\n    - (AB)^-1 = B^-1 * A^-1\n    - (kA)^-1 = k^-1 * A^-1\n\n3) Symmetric Matrix:\n    - A^T = A\n    - For any matrix, A * A^T is always symmetric\n    - For any REAL values matrix A:\n        A + A^T is always Symmetric\n        A - A^T is always Skew-Symmetric\n\n4) Skew - Symmetric:\n    - A^T = -A\n\nA = 1/2(A + A^T + A - A^T). \n\n5) Orthogonal Matrix:\n    - is a square matrix whose columns and rows are Perpendicular vectors. and length of each vector is ONE. The columns form an ORTHONORMAL set.\n    - QQ^T = I = Q^TQ\n    - Q^T = Q^-1\n\n6) Idempotent matrix:\n    - A^2 = A\n\n7) Involuntary Matrix:\n    - A^2 = I  <=>  A = A^-1\n\n8) Nilpotent Matrix:\n    - A^k = 0, where k > 0\n\n9) Hermitian Matrix:\n    - A* = A\n    - A* = (A')^T\n    - A real symmetric matrix is Hermitian\n\n10) Skew-Hermitian:\n    - A* = -A",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Eigen Values and Eigen Vectors\n\n-> when a matrix acts on space, it usually stretches, squishes and rotates vectors off their original line\n    of direction. Eigen values and Eigen vectors identify the rare, invariant directions where no rotation\n    occurs.\n\n-> For Almost every vector x in a space, applying a transformation Ax knocks the vector onto a completely\n    new line of span. However, every transformation has special directions where vectors stay on their exact\n    original line of span.\n\n-> The vectors that stay on their line are eigenvectors.\n-> The factor by which these vectors stretch, shrink, flip or stay unchanged is the eigen value.\n\n-> Ax = lambda*x   =>   (A - lambda*I)x = 0  => det(A - lambda*I) = 0.\n-> Solve the polynomial for lambda. substitute each lambda in (A - lambda*I)x = 0. solve to get eigen vectors corresponding\n    to that lambda. i.e. The resulting basis vectors of this null space form the eigen space for the substituted eigen value.\n\n\n-> As long as the transformation doesn't rotate all the axis of the space in the same direction. A matrix\n    will always have infinte number of eigen vectors.\n\n-> Given the following characteristic equation:\n    (lambda - 1)^5 (lambda - 4)^2 (lambda - 5)^3 = 0\n    \n    - The eigen vectors of two different lambdas are always linearly independent.\n    - For a repeating lambda, the number of linearly indepedent eigen vectors is atmost the power of \n      it's component in the characteristic equation.\n    - So, In total for the matrix with this characteristic equation, there can be atmost 10 LI eigen vectors.\n      and atleast 3 LI eigen vectors. atmost n and atleast # distinct eigen values.\n    \n-> For every eigenvalue lambda, Geometric Multiplicity (GM) is the # LI eigen vectors corresponding to lambda.\n    Algebraic Multiplicity (AM) is the number of times lambda is repeating. GM <= AM.\n\n-> REAL Symmetric Matrices always have AM = GM, so they have n LI eigen vectors which are also orthogonal. The number of distinct eigen values don't matter.\n\n-> Determinant of a matrix A, is equal to the product of eigen values(including repeated values)\n-> Sum of elements in main diagonal of A, trace(A) is equal to sum of eigen values(including repeated values)\n\n-> if zero is an eigen value of a matrix A, Then product of eigen values = det(A). Which implies Det(A) = 0\n-> We can only tell whether the rank of matrix A is full or not from the info of eigen values, that too from the\n    specific info of whether zero is an eigen value of the matrix or not.\n-> But if we were given the GEMOETRIC MULTIPLICTY of lambda = 0, then the rank of matrix a is, (n - GM of zero eigen value)\n    This is because solving (A - lambda*I)x = 0, for lamdba = 0, gives the dimensions of null space of A, which is same \n    as number of LI eigen vectors for zero eigen value.\n\n\nCayley-Hamilton Theorem:\n-> It says, Every square matrix satisfies it's own characteristic equation.\n-> You can use, this to find powers of matrices, even inverse powers. \n\n-> AB and BA share same non zero eigen values, in case they are non-square, if they are square they have same eigen values.\n-> If v is an eigen vector of AB with eigenvalue != 0, then Bv is the eigen vector of BA.\n-> If u is an eigen vector of BA with eigenvalue != 0, then Au is the eigen vector of AB.\n\n-> If A has an eigen value lambda, then A^k has an eigen value lamda^k, where k is an integer.\n\n\nChange of Basis:\n\n-> We have seen that a linear transformation represents, change in the input space of vectors. this change is because of the change in basis vectors. And so far, the matrix A always contains the basis \n    which is represented in terms of standard basis.\n-> So, we can think of the transformation matrix, A as taking inputs of vector space, whose basis vectors are standard basis, and outputting vectors after the transformation\n    which are also represented in terms of standard basis.\n-> Since, we are so accustomed to these kind of transformations, we got blind about the fact that a matrix can take vectors which are represented by non standard basis\n    and can also output vectors in terms of bases which are different from the bases of input vectors.\n\n-> So, A matrix M, which take inputs from bases B and outputs the vectors in terms of bases B' would be of the form:\n    B'[M]B. The columns of M are the transformed basis of B. so columns are represented in terms of bases of B'.\n    The opposite of this, a transformation that takes inputs from B' and converts them to B, would be B[inverse of M]B'.\n\n-> How about representing transformation matrices themselves in terms of other bases?\n    - Let M be a transformation matrix that takes inputs from vector space B and outputs vectors in terms of same basis. We can represent this matrix interms of a different bases P.\n    - Then P M P^-1 represents the transformation matrix interms of bases of P. P^-1 has columns that are basis of P represented interms of basis of B, and P has columns which are basis of B \n      represented interms of P.\n    - for a given vector v in basis of P, when multiplied by above product of matrices, these operations are performed:\n      1) v is converted to the form of bases of M.\n      2) Then the transformation M is performed on the vector.\n      3) This transformed vector is converted back to the basis of P.\n\n    - So, the product of matrices itself gives the transformation matrix interms of bases of P.\n\n\nDiagonalization:\n\n-> Consider a transformation Anxn, if it has n eigen vectors. We can do something interesting.\n-> Note that the transformation doesn't change the direction of lines along the eigen vectors. So, if we represent the transformation in terms of \n    n eigen vectors, i.e. change the baisis to eigenspace, the transformation is just stretching/shrinking the eigen basis. There is no cross talk from one\n    axis/basis to other.\n-> So, we can say, A = Q^-1 D Q, This is what's happening:\n    - Q has columns that are standard basis represented interms of eigen basis. so input vector is first converted into eigenbasis.\n    - Then the transformation is applied by multiplying the input vector with D.\n    - Q^-1 has columns that are eigen basis represented in terms of standard basis. so input vector is converted back into standard basis.\n-> This can also be used to find powers of A, A^k = Q^-1 D^k Q\n\n\nLU Decomposition (Factorization):\n\n-> Matrix A, is represented as the product of a lower triangular matrix and upper triangular matrix.\n    A = LU, the diagonal elements of L are all 1.\n-> Finding Ax = b. It takes O(n^3) to convert A|b into row echelon form. So, everytime you want to find a solution for bi,\n    this needs to be performed.\n\n-> We can optimize this by finding L and U once, which takes O(n^3). And then solve two \n    triangular systems using forward substitution. Ux = y and Ly = b.\n-> Solving triangular systems takes only O(n^2) steps.\n\n-> Determinants can also be calculated fast. det(A) = det(L)*det(U)\n-> Inv(A) = Inv(U) * Inv(L)\n\nSteps to find LU decomposition:\n\n-> U is the resultant matrix after converting A into row echelon form.\n-> L's lower half is determined as follows:\n    - every time you make an element in the lower half of A, zero, you do the following\n      operations: Ri -> Ri + kRj. \n    - Insert k as the element in L at the same place as the element that became zero.\n\n\nSpecial Matrices:\n\n1) Transpose:\n    - (A^-1)^T = (A^T)^-1\n    - (A + B)^T = A^T + B^T\n    - (AB)^T = B^T * A^T\n    - (kA)^T = k * A^T\n\n2) Inverse:\n    - (AB)^-1 = B^-1 * A^-1\n    - (kA)^-1 = k^-1 * A^-1\n\n3) Symmetric Matrix:\n    - A^T = A\n    - For any matrix, A * A^T is always symmetric\n    - For any REAL values matrix A:\n        A + A^T is always Symmetric\n        A - A^T is always Skew-Symmetric\n\n4) Skew - Symmetric:\n    - A^T = -A\n\nA = 1/2(A + A^T + A - A^T). \n\n5) Orthogonal Matrix:\n    - is a square matrix whose columns and rows are Perpendicular vectors. and length of each vector is ONE. The columns form an ORTHONORMAL set.\n    - QQ^T = I = Q^TQ\n    - Q^T = Q^-1\n\n6) Idempotent matrix:\n    - A^2 = A\n\n7) Involuntary Matrix:\n    - A^2 = I  <=>  A = A^-1\n\n8) Nilpotent Matrix:\n    - A^k = 0, where k > 0\n\n9) Hermitian Matrix:\n    - A* = A\n    - A* = (A')^T\n    - A real symmetric matrix is Hermitian\n\n10) Skew-Hermitian:\n    - A* = -A",
